# Zyko's PC Checker 4 — Privacy Policy

Effective September 20, 2026. Applies to version 4.1.0.

## What an examination accesses

Standard scans examine accessible file metadata and relevant technical content,
running processes, installed software, execution traces, Roblox configuration and
logs, account identifiers, download records, Windows history and removable-device
artifacts. File paths, usernames, account IDs, hashes and timestamps can identify
people or reveal their activity. Ordinary personal documents and media receive
limited type checks; a disguised executable may receive deeper examination.

Expanded collection is optional and requires an acknowledgement before each
scan. It additionally collects process command lines and parent relationships,
service and driver settings, scheduled task actions, DNS cache entries, WMI
persistence settings, available Amcache records, restore-point metadata, Windows
event messages, PowerShell command history and bounded NTFS journal/file-reference
records. These sources can reveal private activity. Command lines and saved
commands may contain secrets entered by other software or by a person; automatic
redaction is not guaranteed. Do not enable this scope without appropriate authority.

The separate memory option reads accessible committed readable Roblox process
pages, including non-executable pages, for technical string and PE indicators.
It retains matching indicator names, region metadata and chunk hashes, not raw
memory dumps. Retained loader unload records can also be examined. Protected
memory remains inaccessible. Optional Windows integrity verification invokes
SFC /verifyonly; it performs no repairs but Windows may write diagnostic logs.

Expanded scans can read Chromium/Firefox browsing history and Chromium cache keys,
and decode acquired Amcache, SRUM and MFT files selected by the operator. These
sources can reveal URLs, application use, SIDs, file history and private activity.
Evidence gaps or anomalies do not establish misconduct.

Bundled and custom YARA rules inspect eligible executable and script files locally.
Local custom-rule includes are restricted to the selected rule directory. Rule
names and tags are reported; do not place private information in them.

## Storage, sharing and network connections

Collected inventory and findings remain in application memory until the session
ends unless the operator exports a JSON or HTML report. Exports include the
expanded inventory when enabled, collection limitations and a source timeline.
JSON and HTML exports are unencrypted. The optional .zykoenc export uses password-based authenticated encryption; the app does not retain or recover passwords. Decrypting to JSON writes an unencrypted copy at the selected location. The local scan cache under
`%LOCALAPPDATA%/ZykoPCChecker/scan-cache.sqlite3` retains file paths, identity
metadata, hashes and cached analysis to accelerate later scans. A configured
custom cache path takes precedence. Closing the app does not delete this cache. Optional checkpoint.sqlite3 retains
completed file findings, suppression details, paths and file identity metadata
for resumption. The cache also retains journal/application comparison snapshots.
update-state.sqlite3 stores the version high-water mark. Configured signed feeds
use trusted-feed.json and trusted-rules.sqlite3 for configuration and signed
bundles. These local databases are not password encrypted.

The app does not automatically upload scan evidence, history, memory samples or
reports. Its update checks and downloads contact the configured release service;
that service receives normal network metadata, such as the connecting IP address. A configured rule-feed service also receives update requests. No rule-feed request is made without configured trust.
Windows signature validation may use Windows-managed certificate services.
Sharing an exported report is a separate action controlled by the operator.

## Third-party software acknowledgements and services

This product includes software developed by SecureAuth Corporation
(https://www.secureauth.com/) and Fortra (https://www.fortra.com). It uses the
read-only ESE parser and structure utilities from Impacket 0.13.1 under its
modified Apache Software License 1.1. The prepared runtime excludes Impacket's
networking modules and command-line tools.

Other components include Python, Tcl/Tk, cryptography, cffi, pycparser, pefile,
yara-python/YARA, six, and ppdeep by Marcin Ulikowski, based on SpamSum by Andrew
Tridgell. PyInstaller packages the executable. See THIRD_PARTY.md for attribution
and the release's bundled `licenses/` files for applicable license terms,
copyright notices, disclaimers and exceptions. The app's About, terms & privacy
dialog also displays the third-party notices and bundled license texts.
Third-party licenses govern their respective components and take precedence over
conflicting product restrictions; this policy does not replace those licenses.
Acknowledgement does not imply sponsorship, affiliation or endorsement.

Bundled libraries process data locally for the features described above. Their
inclusion does not itself send scan evidence to their authors. Network services
are separate from these local components:

- **GitHub release services:** Update checks request release information from
  GitHub's API for `Kenxiz/Zyko-s-PC-Checker---Releases`; downloads request release
  assets and checksums through GitHub and its delivery infrastructure. Requests
  expose the connecting IP address, requested URL, request time and HTTP headers,
  including a User-Agent identifying the product and its installed version.
  These requests do not attach scan evidence or reports. GitHub's handling of
  service data is described in its
  [Privacy Statement](https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement).
- **Configured rule-feed providers:** When a trusted feed is configured, the app
  requests rule bundles from that HTTPS URL. The provider and any redirect or
  delivery service receive connection metadata, the requested URL and a
  User-Agent identifying the rule updater. These requests do not attach scan
  evidence. Information or tokens placed in the configured URL are also sent;
  do not put scan evidence or personal identifiers in it. The operator must
  identify the provider and review its privacy terms before configuring a feed.
- **Windows certificate services:** Signature and certificate-chain checks may
  contact Microsoft or certificate-authority endpoints to retrieve certificates
  or revocation status. Depending on the check, endpoints may receive connection
  metadata and certificate identifiers. These are Windows-managed requests, not
  report uploads. Microsoft's practices are described in its
  [Privacy Statement](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/privacy/privacystatement);
  other certificate providers have their own policies.
- **Operator-selected recipients:** If an operator sends a report through email,
  messaging, a support channel or cloud storage, those recipients and services
  receive the shared content. Saving reports or caches in a synchronized folder
  can also cause other software to upload them. Review recipients, access controls
  and service policies before sharing or choosing such a location.

External providers may retain service logs or process data in other countries
under their own policies. Local deletion does not delete their records; consult
the relevant provider for retention, contact details and available privacy
requests. This acknowledgement is not blanket consent to share data, does not
waive privacy rights, and does not remove obligations imposed by applicable law
on the publisher or operator. Before examining another person's device, disclose
the applicable services and recipients as part of the examination scope.

## Retention and control

You may decline expanded collection, stop a running scan or close the app. A
cancelled report can contain evidence already collected. Delete unwanted exported
reports and, after closing the app, the local cache, optional checkpoint and their SQLite sidecar files
to remove application-retained data. Other recipients and operating-system backups
may retain copies; the app cannot delete those remotely. There is no application
cloud account or central evidence store in this build.

Operators must restrict report access, choose and communicate a retention period,
and provide an appropriate contact for questions or deletion requests before
collecting another person's data. The application does not know the operator's
contact details and cannot answer requests on their behalf.

## Accuracy and changes

Collection is broader in version 4, but a greater amount of personal data does
not guarantee better detection. Contextual activity is not proof of misconduct.
Coverage records distinguish unavailable, partial and unsupported checks.
Future changes to collection require corresponding policy and interface updates.
